{
  "id": "maximum-depth-of-binary-tree",
  "title": "Maximum Depth of Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "categories": ["tree", "dfs"],
  "descriptionMd": "You are given the `root` of a binary tree. Its **depth** is the number of nodes on the longest path that starts at the root and goes down to some leaf.\n\nReturn that depth. An empty tree has depth `0`.\n\n**Example 1**\n\n```\nroot = [3, 9, 20, null, null, 15, 7]\n→ 3\n```\nThe path 3 → 20 → 15 (or 3 → 20 → 7) touches three nodes.\n\n**Example 2**\n\n```\nroot = [1, null, 2]\n→ 2\n```",
  "constraints": ["0 ≤ number of nodes ≤ 10⁴", "-100 ≤ Node.val ≤ 100"],
  "hints": ["If you already knew the depth of both subtrees, how would you get the depth of the whole tree?", "Alternatively, process the tree one level at a time and count the levels."],
  "signature": {
    "functionName": "maxDepth",
    "params": [{ "name": "root", "type": "TreeNode" }],
    "returns": "int"
  },
  "comparator": "exact",
  "tests": [
